  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (2250) and comparing each lottery based on the probability of exceeding this level. Lottery B has a higher probability (0.2%) of exceeding 2250 than Lottery A (0.1%), so the reason would favor B. However, the individual chooses A and justifies this by emphasizing the higher reward and downplaying the small probability difference. There is no indication in the verbal report that the individual computed or used any form of average outcome as a reference point, nor did they assess which lottery is more likely to exceed such a threshold. The information considered by the individual (magnitude of prize, perceived insignificance of small probability differences) does not align with the information required by the decision reason (outcomes relative to aspiration level, probability of exceeding it). The justification (preferring higher reward despite lower chance due to already low probabilities) is inconsistent with the rule of the decision reason, which would favor B. Therefore, there is no evidence the individual used the aspiration level reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
